                        HERALDS OF THE CEMÍ



   He chewed a tostón for a while.
   “So I’m a wizard.”
   “You’re a drummer who can hold a room.”
   “That’s a wizard.”
   “It’s not a wizard.”
   “What’s a wizard, then?”
   I had no good answer, which is how most of my conversations with
Manny end. “Somebody who does it on purpose.”
   “Okay, then I’m a wizard who does it by accident.” He thought
about that, and a smile came up slowly, like a tide. “That’s better, actu‐
ally.”
   He wasn’t angry. I’d braced for it. I’d expected him to say what a
person says when you tell him the thing he worked twenty years for
was a birthright. Instead he looked out over the avenue and said, in the
voice he uses for true things:
   “I knew.”
   “What?”
   “Not the big stuff. Not the stone. But the rest.” He turned a tostón
over. “When I was nine I hit a trash can with two pencils and a grown
man stopped on the sidewalk, you remember? I didn’t think it was
weird. I thought that’s what trash cans were for. I thought everybody
could do that. I thought the world was just nicer to me than it was to
you.” He shrugged his huge shoulders. “Babies stop crying. At every
funeral, the room breathes when I play. My tía used to hand me colicky
kids like they were soup. I figured I was lucky. I didn’t figure I was
something.”
   I sat there with my mouth a little open.
   Then I did the stupid thing. I said it the way I’d been rehearsing
since Santo Domingo.
   “I need you out of this.”
   He stopped chewing.
